Three details worth flagging:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10marity[0m is declared [38;5;10mint arity(int, int*)[0m in the header but [3mdefined[23m as [38;5;10munsigned char len[0m in the [38;5;10m.c[0m. GCC compiles the callee to test only the low byte ([38;5;10mcmp $0x1,%dil[0m), so I declared the Rust parameter as [38;5;10mc_int[0m and masked it to 8 bits. That reproduces the truncation for callers going through either signature — verified with [38;5;10mlen[0m values like 256/257/512 which the C treats as 0/1/0.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcompare_allocations[0m compares two [38;5;10mmalloc[0m return addresses and reads back through an aliased pointer. I call the platform [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m directly rather than emulating, so the pointer ordering observes real allocator behavior. This matters: my first differential harness loaded both libraries into one process and reported ~20k mismatches, which was purely glibc tcache free-list state being flipped by the C call before the Rust call. Comparing in separate processes — the actual "same inputs" condition — shows exact agreement.[0m[0m
- All signed arithmetic uses explicit [38;5;10mwrapping_*[0m ops so overflow wraps like GCC's two's-complement output instead of panicking. No bugs were fixed: the uninitialized-then-aliased pointer, the missing NULL checks, and the header/definition type mismatch are all preserved.[0m[0m
